A-S107 / S107 — two-owned-origin CORS rehearsal
Training rehearsal - synthetic data - not a live Azure result

LOCAL CONDUCTING (no packages or Azure needed)
1. Open index.html directly to check an authored policy prediction. The education
   verifier may pass/fail; actual CORS and user impact MUST remain unknown.
2. For genuine HTTP-origin observations, from the repository root in PowerShell:
     node .\learning\S107\serve.cjs
   Open BOTH printed frontend URLs, A and B. Do not substitute URLs or hostnames.
   The API and two frontends are core Node http listeners on literal 127.0.0.1,
   each with its actual ephemeral port. Nothing binds publicly or uses DNS/TLS.
3. On both pages select Baseline v1, predict readable, and send the request.
   Repeat with Compatible v2. Both existing origins should continue reading.
4. Select Incompatible v2. A stays readable. On B predict CORS-denied: education
   passes, but user impact is interrupted. The API sends HTTP 200 without B's
   allow-origin header; the real browser cannot read its fictional profile.
5. Return B to Compatible v2. Recovery does not erase attempt 3. Also try a
   mistaken prediction or deliberately missing observation. Inspect the trace,
   counts and retained journal. Reset restores controls, not history.
6. Each page has six attempts; the server retains at most twelve finished
   receipts and refuses duplicate IDs. Reload ends the client journal, not the
   server ledger. A new fixture is a new run, never a replacement for old proof.
7. Press Ctrl+C in the owning terminal when done. All three owned listeners also
   close automatically after 15 minutes. No server is left running by tests.

WHAT THE RECEIPT DOES AND DOES NOT DO
Same-origin /receipt exposes only the API's actual Origin, finished HTTP status,
allow-origin header, policy/attempt identity and payload hash/length. It never
returns the profile, grants access to the denied response or fetches/proxies it.
The API's full response headers and request target are separately recorded by
the automated fixture. Native proof corroborates these with browser-readable or
blocked fetch results and Chromium's actual CORS diagnostic. A bare TypeError,
painted flag, Node request, CSP denial or missing transport is not CORS proof.
The model has no empirical certification authority, including for HTTP mode.

FOCUSED VERIFICATION (existing tools only; default READ-ONLY)
  node --test .\learning\S107\tests\model.test.cjs .\learning\S107\tests\server.test.cjs .\learning\S107\tests\surface.test.cjs .\learning\S107\tests\integrity.test.cjs
  node .\learning\S107\scripts\syntax.cjs
  node .\learning\S107\scripts\browser.cjs

Capture ONLY when intentionally preserving a new observation:
  $env:CAPTURE_BROWSER_EVIDENCE='1'
  node .\learning\S107\scripts\browser.cjs
  Remove-Item Env:\CAPTURE_BROWSER_EVIDENCE
Exact "1" is required; "true" is not capture. Captures use new immutable paths,
including original failures, and never replace historical reports. Raw RED,
source CRLF, canonical Git LF, browser/server observations and tested source
snapshots live in evidence\raw outside test autodiscovery. integrity.json pins
the publication's bytes; a later deliberate capture intentionally needs a new
reviewed integrity index, not automatic mutation during normal verification.

NECESSARY TEST-ONLY PROTOCOL DEVIATION
Read testing\browser\USAGE.txt. Its published openIsolatedLoopback API intercepts
same-origin requests and buffers/fulfills responses. It cannot qualify native
cross-origin CORS. It was not widened, bypassed, edited or used for this proof.
scripts\native.cjs instead launches fresh sandboxed Edge using only the existing
approved public Microsoft Playwright 1.56.1 distributions and signed installed
Edge 156.0.4314.50. scripts\pins.cjs reads exact approved public cache SHA-512
digests, compares all 510 installed package files with those cached tarballs,
and verifies Edge's Microsoft Authenticode signature before importing Playwright.
No dependency install, browser download, TLS weakening or extension code copy.

The public launchServer/process API gives an exact caller-owned browser handle
for bounded cleanup. Its control transport binds a separate ephemeral literal
127.0.0.1 endpoint; it is automation IPC, not an application origin or proxy.
All application HTTP travels natively: no routes, response fulfillment, proxy
fetch, CORS bypass, cookies, credentials, arbitrary URL input or unsafe flag.
Only the three actual listener origins and fixed owned files can be navigated.
Caller-owned pages use exact-origin CSP and fixed assets/actions; request events
audit that allowlist. This is not a general-purpose runner or OS network sandbox.
The context blocks service workers and does not bypass CSP. Each browser has an
exclusive S107-owned scratch directory; close runs in finally, uses only public
owned handles, confirms process exit, and removes only that literal directory.
Setup/actions and shutdown are bounded. The failed shared lifecycle prototype
is NOT copied or revived. Broader lifecycle qualification remains false.

SCOPE AND REMAINDER
One original frozen S unit in SC-S107. Fixture, helper, repairs, proof and tests
earn no extra credit. All policies/payloads/business data are fictional.
This is real local native-browser CORS evidence, NOT native Azure/cloud evidence.
B-S107, C092/P006 and the full D-S107 leased/native acceptance remain gated by
their original approvals and target/tooling requirements. No infrastructure,
identity, credentials, deployment, system configuration or spending is changed.
Node 22.23.4 is test tooling, not production Node 24 qualification.
